    <p class="plate-note">Because the global residue theorem fixes R₁ + R<sub>½</sub> + R₀ + R<sub>∞</sub>
      = 0 with R<sub>∞</sub> = &minus;1, the three positive residues must sum to one. In this account the
      percentages are not adjustable: they are the algebraic fingerprints of a universe that
      balances.</p>
  </figure>

  <div class="wrap narrow">
    <div class="frame">
      <h3>The numbers it produces</h3>
      <ul class="biblio">
        <li><strong>Ω<sub>Λ</sub> = 68.10%</strong> &mdash; dark energy, the pole at z = 1, π-level.</li>
        <li><strong>Ω<sub>DM</sub> = 26.95%</strong> &mdash; dark matter, the pole at z = ½, π-level but
          uncomplexified.</li>
        <li><strong>Ω<sub>b</sub> = 4.95%</strong> &mdash; baryonic matter, the pole at z = 0, the
          complexified π/2-level.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="muted">Imposing closure quantises κ to a single value. Change κ and the residues stop
        summing to one; in the framework's reading, such a universe could not exist. That is the
        strongest move in the whole volume &mdash; and the one most exposed, because it turns on the
        particular f that was chosen.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

