<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">8.5 and Chapter 9 &mdash; the χ-field</p>
    <h2 id="t-chi">The Modified Einstein Equations</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="wrap narrow">
    <div class="frame illuminated">
      <p>A continuous scalar <strong>integration field</strong> χ(x<sup>μ</sup>) is introduced into the
        Einstein&ndash;Hilbert action, giving</p>
      <p class="formula" style="text-align:center">G<sub>μν</sub> + Λ₀&thinsp;F(χ)&thinsp;g<sub>μν</sub>
        = 8πG&thinsp;T<sub>μν</sub>(χ)</p>
      <p>in which Λ₀ is the <em>unsuppressed</em> quantum vacuum energy &mdash; the ~10<sup>120</sup>
        over-estimate &mdash; and F(χ) is a topological screening function acting as a category block,
        scaling internal quantum fluctuations down to the observed background curvature, with
        F(χ) &#8776; 10<sup>&minus;122</sup> as χ &rarr; 0. The stress-energy tensor then partitions the
        phases by the same residues: T<sub>μν</sub>(χ) = (1 &minus; χ)&thinsp;T<sup>DM</sup><sub>μν</sub>
        + χ&thinsp;T<sup>b</sup><sub>μν</sub>. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
      <h3>Two benchmarks the ansatz claims</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Mercury.</strong> In dense baryonic zones the field saturates (χ &#8776; 1) and
          F(χ) &rarr; 0, so the metric reduces to a modified Schwarzschild geometry with a correction
          below 10<sup>&minus;7</sup> &mdash; preserving the classical 43.0849 arcseconds per century.
          <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> for the benchmark, <span class="spec">&#9670;</span> for the
          reduction.</li>
        <li><strong>The CMB acoustic peaks.</strong> At z &#8776; 1100 the fixed ratio
          Ω<sub>DM</sub>/Ω<sub>b</sub> &#8776; 5.44 drives the photon&ndash;baryon oscillations, placing
          the first peak near &#8467; &#8776; 219, with the second and third set by the baryonic fraction
          and the dark-matter inertia. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--creed">
    <p class="kicker">8.6 &mdash; written by the author against himself</p>
    <h2 id="t-open">Two Open Challenges to a Complete Proof</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Uniqueness of f(z).</strong> Proving that this particular meromorphic function is the
        only mathematically permissible one when algorithmic (Solomonoff) induction is lifted onto the
        complex plane. Until that is done, the function is a choice that happens to work.</li>
      <li><strong>A differential derivation of κ.</strong> Deriving κ = 1/4π² directly from a fundamental
        topological invariant &mdash; a Chern number or an Euler characteristic of a four-dimensional
        manifold undergoing Möbius inversion &mdash; so that no appearance of post-hoc tuning
        remains.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="muted">Both are stated in the Unified Edition itself. A framework that lists its own two
      unpaid debts in the same chapter as its headline result is doing something most cosmologies do
      not, and the site keeps that on the page rather than in a footnote.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Earned</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>The global residue theorem, and the arithmetic of the normalisation.
          <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></li>
        <li>That the three stated residues do sum to one once R<sub>∞</sub> = &minus;1 is imposed.
          <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></li>
        <li>That the measured cosmological budget is close to these figures.
          <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Chosen</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>f(z) itself, and the identification of each pole with a cosmic component.
          <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></li>
        <li>κ as the torus-to-Möbius ratio rather than as a number that lands in the right place.
          <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></li>
        <li>F(χ) as the screening mechanism; its form is motivated, not derived.
          <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--creed">
    <p class="kicker">Closing Stance</p>
    <p>This is where the framework becomes checkable, and so where it becomes vulnerable. The right test
      is not whether the percentages match &mdash; they were known before the function was written
      &mdash; but whether f(z) can be shown to be forced. Until the uniqueness proof exists, the Ledger
      is an elegant account of numbers already in hand.</p>
